---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.6 (Day 1) — Dungeon Crawler RPG Build: Combat &amp; Duel Engine</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.9 · 90-Minute RPG Build (Day 1 of 2).</strong> Over the next two
    classes you will build <code>DungeonLoop.java</code> — combining indeterminate
    <code>while</code> loops (for combat rounds until one fighter falls) with fixed-count
    <code>for</code> loops (for rooms and multi-floor campaigns). Today you complete Parts 1 and 2;
    next class (<code>4.6b</code>) you build floor exploration and the multi-floor campaign.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Spec-First Workflow (Download All Three Files):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/DungeonLoop.java" download>DungeonLoop.java</a> — starter stub for
            both days of the 4.6 build.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/DungeonLoopCheck.java" download>DungeonLoopCheck.java</a> — final
            acceptance verification suite (13 checks across Parts 1–4; Day 1 covers the 6 checks in
            Parts 1–2).
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/Dice.java" download>Dice.java</a> — your dice engine from Unit 1.7.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong> Read each specification, analyze the edge cases
        (one-shot kills, overkill clamping, natural 1/20 priority, dead-monster guards), draft the
        code pieces in the prompts below, assemble them in <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>, and test
        your own edge cases <em>before</em> running <code>DungeonLoopCheck</code>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java DungeonLoop.java DungeonLoopCheck.java
java DungeonLoopCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Spec, Edge Cases &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>simulateDuelToDeath(int playerHP, int playerDmgPerHit, int monsterHP, int monsterDmgPerHit)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Before adding dice randomness, build the deterministic duel engine that tomorrow's floor
        crawler will also call as a helper:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Loop <strong>while both combatants are alive</strong>
            (<code>playerHP &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; monsterHP &gt; 0</code>).</li>
        <li>In each round:
            <ol>
                <li>The player strikes first: subtract <code>playerDmgPerHit</code> from
                    <code>monsterHP</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Only if <code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code></strong>, the monster strikes back:
                    subtract <code>monsterDmgPerHit</code> from <code>playerHP</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>
        <li>When the loop ends, return the player's remaining HP — <strong>clamped at
            <code>0</code></strong> if <code>playerHP</code> dropped below <code>0</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>(20, 6, 12, 5)</code> &rarr; R1: monster <code>6</code>, player <code>15</code>; R2: monster <code>0</code> (dead, no counterattack!) &rarr; returns <code>15</code>.</li>
                <li><code>(10, 15, 12, 99)</code> &rarr; R1: monster <code>-3</code> (one-shot kill, monster never swings!) &rarr; returns <code>10</code>.</li>
                <li><code>(10, 3, 20, 6)</code> &rarr; R1: m <code>17</code>, p <code>4</code>; R2: m <code>14</code>, p <code>-2</code> &rarr; clamped to <code>0</code>.</li>
                <li><code>(18, 5, 0, 10)</code> &rarr; monster is already dead, loop runs <code>0</code> times &rarr; returns <code>18</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: Loop Condition, Zombie Swings &amp; Overkill Negatives</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if a student writes
                <code>while (playerHP &gt; 0 || monsterHP &gt; 0)</code> ("keep fighting while
                someone is alive") instead of <code>&amp;&amp;</code>? Trace what happens after the
                monster dies on Round 2 while the player still has <code>15 HP</code>!</li>
            <li>Apply De Morgan's Law: the duel stops when
                <code>playerHP &lt;= 0 || monsterHP &lt;= 0</code>. Negate that stop condition to
                prove why the continuation condition must use <code>&amp;&amp;</code>?</li>
            <li>What two bugs happen if you omit (a) <code>if (monsterHP &gt; 0)</code> before the
                monster's attack and (b) clamping <code>playerHP</code> at <code>0</code> after the
                loop?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>simulateDuelToDeath</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Duel Loop &amp; Turn Order):</strong> Write the
                <code>while</code> loop header and the exact two-step round body (player strike,
                then guarded monster counterattack)?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Overkill Clamping &amp; Return):</strong> Write the post-loop
                clamping return statement so negative player HP returns <code>0</code> while
                <code>(18, 5, 0, 10)</code> returns <code>18</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B inside <code>simulateDuelToDeath</code> in
            <code>DungeonLoop.java</code> and hand-trace <code>(10, 15, 12, 99)</code> and
            <code>(10, 3, 20, 6)</code>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Live Dice Combat</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>runCombat(...)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        Now implement the live dice-driven combat loop using <code>Dice.roll(...)</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>public static boolean runCombat(String playerName, int playerHP, int playerAC,
                                int playerAttackBonus, String monsterName,
                                int monsterHP, int monsterAC,
                                int monsterAttackBonus, int monsterDamageDie)</code></pre>
    <ul>
        <li>Loop while <code>playerHP &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; monsterHP &gt; 0</code>:
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Player attack:</strong> Roll <code>int d20 = Dice.roll(20);</code>. A
                    natural <code>20</code> deals <code>Dice.roll(8) * 2</code> damage (always hits);
                    a natural <code>1</code> deals <code>0</code> damage (always misses); otherwise
                    if <code>d20 + playerAttackBonus &gt;= monsterAC</code>, deal
                    <code>Dice.roll(8)</code> damage. Subtract from <code>monsterHP</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Monster counterattack (only if <code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code>):</strong>
                    Roll <code>int m20 = Dice.roll(20);</code>. If
                    <code>m20 + monsterAttackBonus &gt;= playerAC</code>, subtract
                    <code>Dice.roll(monsterDamageDie)</code> from <code>playerHP</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>
        <li>Return <code>true</code> if <code>playerHP &gt; 0</code> (player won), or
            <code>false</code> otherwise.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: Crit/Fumble Branch Order &amp; Boolean Zen</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Suppose a hero has <code>playerAttackBonus = +20</code> facing a Goblin with
                <code>monsterAC = 8</code>, and rolls <code>d20 = 1</code>. Why would putting
                <code>if (d20 + playerAttackBonus &gt;= monsterAC)</code> <em>above</em>
                <code>else if (d20 == 1)</code> let a Natural 1 hit the Goblin?</li>
            <li>How can you write the final <code>return</code> statement of <code>runCombat</code>
                in a single line using Boolean Zen instead of an <code>if / else</code> returning
                <code>true</code> and <code>false</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>runCombat</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Player Strike Chain):</strong> Inside the combat
                <code>while</code> loop, write the <code>d20</code> roll and ordered
                <code>if / else if / else if</code> chain for Natural <code>20</code>, Natural
                <code>1</code>, and normal AC hit that updates <code>monsterHP</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Guarded Monster Strike &amp; Return):</strong> Write the
                <code>if (monsterHP &gt; 0)</code> counterattack block using
                <code>Dice.roll(monsterDamageDie)</code>, plus the final Boolean Zen
                <code>return</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B inside <code>runCombat</code> in <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>.
            Test both Day 1 methods in a scratch <code>main</code> (checking a one-shot kill,
            overkill clamping, and a live fight), and <strong>only then</strong> run
            <code>java DungeonLoopCheck</code> to verify all 6 Day 1 checks in Parts 1–2 pass.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Day 1 Milestone — Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Parts 1 and 2 in <code>DungeonLoop.java</code> were built from your pieces and self-tested before running the check</li>
            <li>All <strong>6 checks in Parts 1 and 2</strong> of <code>java DungeonLoopCheck</code> report <code>PASS</code> (Parts 3–4 are tomorrow!)</li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection and code-piece boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/dungeon-system.html">Dungeon Loop System (Student RPG Guide)</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/while-loops.html">while Loops</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
